cff-version: 1.2.0
message: >-
  If you use this work, please cite the preprint listed under
  "preferred-citation" below.
title: >-
  Precision Pays: A Fixed-Weight Boundary Loss Anchors the Precision End of a Recall–Precision Axis and Wins as the Consensus Veto under the Official BraTS-2023 Metrics
abstract: >-
  Reproducibility archive for Paper 2 of the BRATS auxiliary-loss program.
  Does the Kervadec boundary loss (fixed weight lambda = 0.05, no auxiliary
  head) added to a strong MedNeXt/nnU-Net glioma backbone improve adult
  glioma segmentation? Under the complete official BraTS-2023 metric set over
  a 5-fold cross-validation on the 1196-case study set (1251 unified cases
  downloaded; the 55 exclusions are measured and listed case by case in
  analysis/DATASET_EXCLUSIONS.md) the answer is no: a transparently reported
  negative result, no significant gain over the baseline on any official
  metric. Its robust distinctive property is being the precision-oriented
  mirror of a signed-distance auxiliary head, which makes it an effective
  connected-component consensus veto. The archive bundles the manuscript
  (EN + FR with both PDFs), the reproducibility code, the exact result JSONs,
  tables, figures, the dataset-provenance artefacts and the references.
